{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-49f4306b-1034-4a9f-b1c5-b0d8619b0328","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7eeeb36dc0d6e80","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9011-fgyq2cyr/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9011-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9011 (Adopt structured JSON logging across RHOAI components)\n\nTriage: not below-threshold (spans 7 components and multiple teams), not\ndocumentation-only (all affected components require code changes). Full DAG\ndecomposition applies.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 | Publish platform structured logging convention (JSON schema, required fields, per-language library guidance) | Implementation | RHOAI Observability | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E002 | Dashboard emits convention-conformant structured logs | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E003 | KServe controller emits convention-conformant structured logs | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E004 | Serving runtimes emit convention-conformant structured logs | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E005 | Data Science Pipelines (operator and API server) emits convention-conformant structured logs | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E006 | Model Registry emits convention-conformant structured logs | Implementation | Model Registry | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E007 | TrustyAI services emit convention-conformant structured logs | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E008 | Notebooks (notebook-controller) emits convention-conformant structured logs | Implementation | Workbenches | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E009 | Training Operator emits convention-conformant structured logs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9011-E010 | Document the structured log format and example support queries | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | P1 |\n\nNo Investigation epics: the strategy's Open Questions section is explicitly\n\"None\" — the convention (HLR-1) is prerequisite design delivered as an\nImplementation epic, and the per-component adoptions are mechanical once it\nlands. No risks table, assumptions, pending reviews, or conditional ADRs exist\nin the strategy.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[E001 Platform logging convention] --> E002[E002 Dashboard adoption]\n    E001 --> E003[E003 KServe controller adoption]\n    E001 --> E004[E004 Serving runtimes adoption]\n    E001 --> E005[E005 Data Science Pipelines adoption]\n    E001 --> E006[E006 Model Registry adoption]\n    E001 --> E007[E007 TrustyAI adoption]\n    E001 --> E008[E008 Notebooks adoption]\n    E001 --> E009[E009 Training Operator adoption]\n    E002 --> E010[E010 Documentation]\n    E003 --> E010\n    E004 --> E010\n    E005 --> E010\n    E006 --> E010\n    E007 --> E010\n    E008 --> E010\n    E009 --> E010\n```\n\nStructure: one P0 design epic at the root, eight mutually parallel adoption\nepics, one docs epic that closes the strategy. Critical path length: 3\n(E001 → any adoption epic → E010).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002..E009 | Rule 13 (artifact → consumer) | The convention (JSON schema, required fields, per-language library guidance) is an artifact each adoption's code builds against; every adoption is serial after it. This also matches the problem statement: adoptions are independent of each other *once the convention exists*. |\n| E002..E009 (no edges between them) | Rules 14/15 | Adoptions live in different repos/components with no shared artifacts; the strategy states they are independent. Merge-conflict/coordination risk is not a dependency. |\n| E001..E009 → E010 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The documentation describes the log format that was actually built and queries over the components that actually emit it, so it is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n| E010 priority P1 (not HLR-9's P2) | Step 4 docs-authoring priority exception | Derived from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. Docs epics do not push priority upstream (the adoption epics keep their HLR priorities). |\n| No tiered delivery | Step 5.5 | No external dependencies with uncertain timing (strategy declares none); all work is in midstream repos the teams control. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E002 |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E003, RHAISTRAT-9011-E004 |\n| HLR-4 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E005 |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E006 |\n| HLR-6 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E007 |\n| HLR-7 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E008 |\n| HLR-8 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E009 |\n| HLR-9 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E010 |\n\nFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements;\nno epic without an HLR. HLR-3 spans two canonical components (KServe and\nServing Runtimes) and is deliberately split into two epics by component\nboundary (epic boundary Rule 1), both covering HLR-3.\n\n## Component and Team Mappings\n\nNon-obvious mappings (canonical component names come verbatim from\n`.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n\n- **E001 (convention)**: the strategy names no owning component or team for the\n  platform-wide convention. Mapped to the canonical `Documentation` component\n  (the deliverable is a published platform specification — there is no\n  platform-wide canonical component) and to an assumed `RHOAI Observability`\n  team (grounded in the strategy's `observability` label). Both need human\n  confirmation — see Health Warnings.\n- **E003/E004 (HLR-3 split)**: HLR-3 names \"KServe controller and serving\n  runtimes\". `KServe` and `Serving Runtimes` are separate canonical components,\n  so the work is split: the controller (KServe) and the model-serving runtimes\n  (Serving Runtimes). Both assigned to RHOAI Serving, the owning team in\n  `architecture-context/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`, which also documents the serving\n  runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) as part of the serving stack.\n- **Serving Runtimes** has no dedicated file under\n  `.context/architecture-context/` but is documented inside\n  `architecture-context/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` as an in-platform dependency\n  (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit for model execution). It is therefore treated as\n  in-platform and gets a normal adoption epic — no repo/konflux onboarding\n  chain is generated.\n- **Adoption epic teams** are taken from each component's architecture-context\n  owning team (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard, KServe/Serving Runtimes → RHOAI\n  Serving, Data Science Pipelines → RHOAI Pipelines, Model Registry → Model\n  Registry, TrustyAI → TrustyAI, Notebooks → Workbenches, Training Operator →\n  Distributed Workloads), because the strategy has no Impacted Teams table.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture context;\n  acceptance criteria below are derived from the HLRs. Non-blocking, but the\n  epic ACs should be sanity-checked by the component teams.\n- **Unowned convention work (HLR-1)**: no team is named for authoring the\n  platform logging convention. `RHOAI Observability` is an assumption — confirm\n  the owning team before scheduling E001, since it gates all eight adoption\n  epics.\n- **Serving Runtimes architecture coverage**: no dedicated architecture-context\n  file exists for the `Serving Runtimes` canonical component (only the KServe\n  file describes it). Treated as in-platform on that evidence; if the runtimes\n  are actually delivered out-of-band, E004's scope needs re-checking.\n- **Scope trap guard**: log storage, retention, and forwarding are explicitly\n  out of scope, so all adoption epic ACs verify the *emitted log format only*\n  (schema conformance at the source), never end-to-end centralized querying.\n  The documentation epic's example queries describe query patterns against the\n  documented format; validating them against a real log platform is out of\n  scope.\n- **Priority note**: the P2 adoption (Training Operator, HLR-8) is genuinely\n  deferrable — independent repo, independent team, no shared surface with the\n  P0/P1 adoptions — so it stays P2 rather than being inflated.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable: no external dependency with uncertain timing. All epics are\nindependent midstream work gated only by the convention epic.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":37676,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a8bc18897112f2d","agent_id":"a8b7c416e92542af2","session_id":"58dc5346-ca06-4598-877d-d8e47ba1db78","uuid":"c6a96398-519d-4076-9dda-5f5233df7b0d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T18:10:22.693Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9011"}
