011-E010 | Document the structured log format and example support queries | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | P1 |\n29\t\n30\tNo Investigation epics: the strategy's Open Questions section is explicitly\n31\t\"None\" — the convention (HLR-1) is prerequisite design delivered as an\n32\tImplementation epic, and the per-component adoptions are mechanical once it\n33\tlands. No risks table, assumptions, pending reviews, or conditional ADRs exist\n34\tin the strategy.\n35\t\n36\t## Dependency DAG\n37\t\n38\t```mermaid\n39\tgraph TD\n40\t    E001[E001 Platform logging convention] --> E002[E002 Dashboard adoption]\n41\t    E001 --> E003[E003 KServe controller adoption]\n42\t    E001 --> E004[E004 Serving runtimes adoption]\n43\t    E001 --> E005[E005 Data Science Pipelines adoption]\n44\t    E001 --> E006[E006 Model Registry adoption]\n45\t    E001 --> E007[E007 TrustyAI adoption]\n46\t    E001 --> E008[E008 Notebooks adoption]\n47\t    E001 --> E009[E009 Training Operator adoption]\n48\t    E002 --> E010[E010 Documentation]\n49\t    E003 --> E010\n50\t    E004 --> E010\n51\t    E005 --> E010\n52\t    E006 --> E010\n53\t    E007 --> E010\n54\t    E008 --> E010\n55\t    E009 --> E010\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\tStructure: one P0 design epic at the root, eight mutually parallel adoption\n59\tepics, one docs epic that closes the strategy. Critical path length: 3\n60\t(E001 → any adoption epic → E010).\n61\t\n62\t## DAG Justification\n63\t\n64\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n65\t|------|------|-----------|\n66\t| E001 → E002..E009 | Rule 13 (artifact → consumer) | The convention (JSON schema, required fields, per-language library guidance) is an artifact each adoption's code builds against; every adoption is serial after it. This also matches the problem statement: adoptions are independent of each other *once the convention exists*. |\n67\t| E002..E009 (no edges between them) | Rules 14/15 | Adoptions live in different repos/components with no shared artifacts; the strategy states they are independent. Merge-conflict/coordination risk is not a dependency. |\n68\t| E001..E009 → E010 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The documentation describes the log format that was actually built and queries over the components that actually emit it, so it is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n69\t| E010 priority P1 (not HLR-9's P2) | Step 4 docs-authoring priority exception | Derived from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. Docs epics do not push priority upstream (the adoption epics keep their HLR priorities). |\n70\t| No tiered delivery | Step 5.5 | No external dependencies with uncertain timing (strategy declares none); all work is in midstream repos the teams control. |\n71\t\n72\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n73\t\n74\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n75\t|-----|----------|---------|\n76\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 |\n77\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E002 |\n78\t| HLR-3 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E003, RHAISTRAT-9011-E004 |\n79\t| HLR-4 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E005 |\n80\t| HLR-5 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E006 |\n81\t| HLR-6 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E007 |\n82\t| HLR-7 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E008 |\n83\t| HLR-8 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E009 |\n84\t| HLR-9 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E010 |\n85\t\n86\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements;\n87\tno epic without an HLR. HLR-3 spans two canonical components (KServe and\n88\tServing Runtimes) and is deliberately split into two epics by component\n89\tboundary (epic boundary Rule 1), both covering HLR-3.\n90\t\n91\t## Component and Team Mappings\n92\t\n93\tNon-obvious mappings (canonical component names come verbatim from\n94\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n95\t\n96\t- **E001 (convention)**: the strategy names no owning component or team for the\n97\t  platform-wide convention. Mapped to the canonical `Documentation` component\n98\t  (the deliverable is a published platform specification — there is no\n99\t  platform-wide canonical component) and to an assumed `RHOAI Observability`\n100\t  team (grounded in the strategy's `observability` label). Both need human\n101\t  confirmation — see Health Warnings.\n102\t- **E003/E004 (HLR-3 split)**: HLR-3 names \"KServe controller and serving\n103\t  runtimes\". `KServe` and `Serving Runtimes` are separate canonical components,\n104\t  so the work is split: the controller (KServe) and the model-serving runtimes\n105\t  (Serving Runtimes). Both assigned to RHOAI Serving, the owning team in\n106\t  `architecture-context/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`, which also documents the serving\n107\t  runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) as part of the serving stack.\n108\t- **Serving Runtimes** has no dedicated file under\n109\t  `.context/architecture-context/` but is documented inside\n110\t  `architecture-context/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` as an in-platform dependency\n111\t  (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit for model execution). It is therefore treated as\n112\t  in-platform and gets a normal adoption epic — no repo/konflux onboarding\n113\t  chain is generated.\n114\t- **Adoption epic teams** are taken from each component's architecture-context\n115\t  owning team (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard, KServe/Serving Runtimes → RHOAI\n116\t  Serving, Data Science Pipelines → RHOAI Pipelines, Model Registry → Model\n117\t  Registry, TrustyAI → TrustyAI, Notebooks → Workbenches, Training Operator →\n118\t  Distributed Workloads), because the strategy has no Impacted Teams table.\n119\t\n120\t## Health Warnings\n121\t\n122\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n123\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n124\t  Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture context;\n125\t  acceptance criteria below are derived from the HLRs. Non-blocking, but the\n126\t  epic ACs should be sanity-checked by the component teams.\n127\t- **Unowned convention work (HLR-1)**: no team is named for authoring the\n128\t  platform logging convention