onents with no shared artifacts; the strategy states they are independent. Merge-conflict/coordination risk is not a dependency. |\n70\t| E001..E009 → E010 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The documentation describes the log format that was actually built and queries over the components that actually emit it, so it is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n71\t| E010 priority P1 (not HLR-9's P2) | Step 4 docs-authoring priority exception | Derived from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. Docs epics do not push priority upstream (the adoption epics keep their HLR priorities). |\n72\t| No tiered delivery | Step 5.5 | No external dependencies with uncertain timing (strategy declares none); all work is in midstream repos the teams control. |\n73\t\n74\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n75\t\n76\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n77\t|-----|----------|---------|\n78\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E001 |\n79\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E002 |\n80\t| HLR-3 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E003, RHAISTRAT-9011-E004 |\n81\t| HLR-4 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E005 |\n82\t| HLR-5 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E006 |\n83\t| HLR-6 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E007 |\n84\t| HLR-7 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E008 |\n85\t| HLR-8 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E009 |\n86\t| HLR-9 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9011-E010 |\n87\t\n88\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements;\n89\tno epic without an HLR. HLR-3 spans two canonical components (KServe and\n90\tServing Runtimes) and is deliberately split into two epics by component\n91\tboundary (epic boundary Rule 1), both covering HLR-3.\n92\t\n93\t## Component and Team Mappings\n94\t\n95\tNon-obvious mappings (canonical component names come verbatim from\n96\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n97\t\n98\t- **E001 (convention)**: the strategy names no owning component or team for the\n99\t  platform-wide convention. Mapped to the canonical `Documentation` component\n100\t  (the deliverable is a published platform specification — there is no\n101\t  platform-wide canonical component) and to an assumed `RHOAI Observability`\n102\t  team (grounded in the strategy's `observability` label). Both need human\n103\t  confirmation — see Health Warnings.\n104\t- **E003/E004 (HLR-3 split)**: HLR-3 names \"KServe controller and serving\n105\t  runtimes\". `KServe` and `Serving Runtimes` are separate canonical components,\n106\t  so the work is split: the controller (KServe) and the model-serving runtimes\n107\t  (Serving Runtimes). Both assigned to RHOAI Serving, the owning team in\n108\t  `architecture-context/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`, which also documents the serving\n109\t  runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) as part of the serving stack.\n110\t- **Serving Runtimes** has no dedicated file under\n111\t  `.context/architecture-context/` but is documented inside\n112\t  `architecture-context/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` as an in-platform dependency\n113\t  (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit for model execution). It is therefore treated as\n114\t  in-platform and gets a normal adoption epic — no repo/konflux onboarding\n115\t  chain is generated.\n116\t- **Adoption epic teams** are taken from each component's architecture-context\n117\t  owning team (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard, KServe/Serving Runtimes → RHOAI\n118\t  Serving, Data Science Pipelines → RHOAI Pipelines, Model Registry → Model\n119\t  Registry, TrustyAI → TrustyAI, Notebooks → Workbenches, Training Operator →\n120\t  Distributed Workloads), because the strategy has no Impacted Teams table.\n121\t\n122\t## Health Warnings\n123\t\n124\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n125\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n126\t  Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from architecture context;\n127\t  acceptance criteria below are derived from the HLRs. Non-blocking, but the\n128\t  epic ACs should be sanity-checked by the component teams.\n129\t- **Unowned convention work (HLR-1)**: no team is named for authoring the\n130\t  platform logging convention. `RHOAI Observability` is an assumption — confirm\n131\t  the owning team before scheduling E001, since it gates all eight adoption\n132\t  epics.\n133\t- **Serving Runtimes architecture coverage**: no dedicated architecture-context\n134\t  file exists for the `Serving Runtimes` canonical component (only the KServe\n135\t  file describes it). Treated as in-platform on that evidence; if the runtimes\n136\t  are actually delivered out-of-band, E004's scope needs re-checking.\n137\t- **Scope trap guard**: log storage, retention, and forwarding are explicitly\n138\t  out of scope, so all adoption epic ACs verify the *emitted log format only*\n139\t  (schema conformance at the source), never end-to-end centralized querying.\n140\t  The documentation epic's example queries describe query patterns against the\n141\t  documented format; validating them against a real log platform is out of\n142\t  scope.\n143\t- **Priority note**: the P2 adoption (Training Operator, HLR-8) is genuinely\n144\t  deferrable — independent repo, independent team, no shared surface with the\n145\t  P0/P1 adoptions — so it stays P2 rather than being inflated.\n146\t\n147\t## Tiered Delivery\n148\t\n149\tNot applicable: no external dependency with uncertain timing. All epics are\n150\tindependent midstream work gated only by the convention epic.\n151\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0f727480bbd78fe","agent_id":"a748cfc1ccfc58396","session_id":"58dc5346-ca06-4598-877d-d8e47ba1db78","uuid":"0abd5774-7a4c-4319-ae6d-80ed8103bb5b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T18:21:17.920Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9759d925ffbe06d8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9011 decomposition"}
